Found the bug in `match`:

```cpp
const std::size_t window = std::min(text.size(), max_len_ + 16);
const std::string_view tail = text.substr(text.size() - window);
const std::size_t at = tail.find(s);
if (at == 0) return text.size() - window + at;
```

Two bugs:
1. It only checks the tail of the text (`window = min(text.size(), max_len_ + 16)`), so a stop string that appears earlier in the text (e.g. at position 20, with the text growing to thousands of chars) is not found.
2. `if (at == 0)` only matches if `s` starts exactly at the beginning of the tail window. So even if the stop string is present in the tail but starts at position > 0, it won't match. It should return when `at != npos`, and return `text.size() - window + at`.

Also `partial_suffix` — let's check the header.

Found the bug. In `json_object_end`, when inside a string and encountering `\\`, it does `continue` which skips just the backslash — the escaped quote is still visited. Actually, `continue` on a `\` means it skips to the next char, i.e., the next char (e.g., `"`) will be visited as a normal char. If the escaped char is `"`, then it will toggle in_string off. The fix: when seeing `\\`, advance i by one (skip the escaped char).

Also in scan_tool_calls, `pos = end` — fine.

Fix:

```cpp
if (in_string) {
    if (c == '\\') { ++i; continue; }  // careful with loop's ++i
    if (c == '"') in_string = false;
    continue;
}
```

With `for (i = begin; i < size; ++i)`, `++i` inside then `continue` means i advances twice. That's fine as long as i < text.size(); if `\` is last char, ++i could skip past — then loop condition i < size fails. Fine.

